[tool-call] delta {"path":"/home/mihailo/git/graalvm-reachability-metadata/forge/local_repositories/forge_prepublication_review_worktrees/review-c641cd33/.forge-local-review-424939d2/verdict.json","content":"{\n  \"decision\": \"rejected\",\n  \"action\": \"human-intervention\",\n  \"review_comment\": \"The contribution is scoped to one coordinate, uses only reachability-metadata.json, has non-scaffold tests in a separate package with no native skips or version-pinned assertions, and reports 24/25 (96%) dynamic-access coverage. It cannot be approved because the required new Couchbase image was not actually vulnerability-scanned: checkAllowedDockerImages returned success after Grype failed. Under §FS-contribution-contract.5.3 and §FS-contribution-contract.5.5, this shared fail-open scanner defect requires maintainer intervention.\",\n  \"finding_title\": \"Docker image vulnerability scan passes when Grype fails\",\n  \"finding_body\": \"The contribution adds couchbase/server:community-7.6.2, but the recorded checkAllowedDockerImages run shows Grype failed to load/update its database and failed to catalog the image, while Gradle still returned 0 with BUILD SUCCESSFUL. tests/tck-build-logic/src/main/java/org/graalvm/internal/tck/GrypeTask.java runs `grype -o json <image> | jq ...` through `/bin/sh` without pipefail or separate Grype exit-status validation, so jq's successful exit masks the scanner failure and the new image's vulnerability baseline remains unverified. This is a shared harness defect, not repairable inside the coordinate's allowed files.\",\n  \"fix_note\": \"\",\n  \"infrastructure_issue\": {\n    \"title\": \"Docker image vulnerability scan passes when Grype fails\",\n    \"body\": \"Cause: `GrypeTask.getVulnerabilities` executes `grype -o json <image> | jq ...` using `/bin/sh` without pipefail or separate validation of Grype's exit status, allowing jq's status to mask a Grype failure. Reproduction: run `./gradlew checkAllowedDockerImages --baseCommit=origin/master --newCommit=HEAD` for a change adding an allowed image while Grype cannot load its database or catalog the image. In the recorded org.springframework.data:spring-data-couchbase:6.1.1 gate, Grype reported a stale/unloadable database and `failed to catalog` for couchbase/server:community-7.6.2, including `no space left on device`, but Gradle reported `BUILD SUCCESSFUL` and returned 0. The image therefore passed without a vulnerability scan.\"\n  }\n}\n"}
